## 2. Do I have to specify every value one by one?

No. Three mechanisms, from ad hoc to fully variable-driven:

**a. Repeatable flags.** Every filter flag can be given multiple times; values within one flag are OR-ed:

```bash
python3 check_ARGO_sync.py -d '<context>' \
    --project snd-linuxadmin --project master-app
```

**b. Discover the values instead of guessing them (§3).** One command lists every distinct value that currently exists — copy from there.

**c. Icinga custom variables (§4).** This is the real answer to "don't hardcode." The values live in **one place** (host vars), Icinga expands them into repeated flags automatically, and adding/removing a project or cluster is a one-line vars edit — no script change, no CheckCommand change.

---

## 3. Discovering current values (nothing hardcoded)

Run these whenever you need the current list. They read live from the API, so they stay correct as projects/clusters/namespaces are added, deleted, or renamed.

**Step 1 — set the context once:**

```bash
CTX='default/api-os4hub-hobbylobby-corp:6443/system:serviceaccount:default:svcicinga'
```

**Step 2 — list distinct projects:**

```bash
oc --context "$CTX" get applications.argoproj.io -n openshift-gitops -o json \
  | jq -r '.items[].spec.project' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
```

**Step 3 — list distinct clusters (name or server URL, whichever is set):**

```bash
oc --context "$CTX" get applications.argoproj.io -n openshift-gitops -o json \
  | jq -r '.items[].spec.destination | .name // .server' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
```

**Step 4 — list distinct destination namespaces:**

```bash
oc --context "$CTX" get applications.argoproj.io -n openshift-gitops -o json \
  | jq -r '.items[].spec.destination.namespace' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
```

**No jq available?** One jsonpath call shows all three columns:

```bash
oc --context "$CTX" get applications.argoproj.io -n openshift-gitops \
  -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.spec.project}{"\t"}{.spec.destination.server}{"\t"}{.spec.destination.namespace}{"\n"}{end}' \
  | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
```

**Step 5 — verify a value before wiring it into Icinga:**

```bash
python3 check_ARGO_sync.py -d "$CTX" --project <value-from-step-2> -v
```

The `-v` output prints `N/1603 applications matched the filter`, confirming the value is live.

